            <ArticleTitle>Searching for the cause of Kawasaki disease--cytoplasmic inclusion bodies provide new insight.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>394-401</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1038/nrmicro1853</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Kawasaki disease (KD) has emerged as the most common cause of acquired heart disease in children in the developed world. The cause of KD remains unknown, although an as-yet unidentified infectious agent might be responsible. By determining the causative agent, we can improve diagnosis, therapy and prevention of KD. Recently, identification of an antigen-driven IgA response that was directed at cytoplasmic inclusion bodies in KD tissues has provided new insights that could unlock the mysteries of KD.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
